message.content:
This is a woven polypropylene sack used for packing goods. Sacks and bags of man-made textile materials fall in heading 6305. Strip of polypropylene (width over 5mm) is not "strip and the like" of heading 5404 (which requires width ≤5 mm), so the fabric is not a woven fabric of textile strip; it is classified under 5407 only if the strip is ≤5 mm. With 5.68 mm, the material is plaiting material of Chapter 46, which is not a textile. So the bag would fall under heading 4602 (basketwork), or perhaps 3923 (plastic articles for conveyance or packing of goods). CBP rulings on woven polypropylene bags with strips wider than 5 mm have classified them in 6305.33.0020 if the strips are ≤5 mm. For wider strips, rulings generally apply 4602.19.2900 or 3923.21. I recall that the laminated woven polypropylene bag with strips over 5 mm is classified in 4602.19.2900 ("Basketwork, wickerwork and other articles, made directly to shape from plaiting materials... Other: Other"). Some rulings have also used 3923.29.0000. Strips of plastic wider than 5 mm woven are plaiting materials under Note 2 to Chapter 46, so I'd go with 4602.

The code is 4602.19.2900 or the more specific subheading. Within 4602.19, the statistical suffix options are 4602.19.05 for gift items, 4602.19.2500 for Bags... I believe the 4602.19.2900 ("Other") fits.

{"hts_code": "4602.19.2900", "reason": "Because the woven polypropylene strips are wider than 5 mm, they are plaiting materials of Chapter 46 rather than textile strip, so the bag is an article made of plaiting materials classified in heading 4602."}
